1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,356
Circuit Studio connects equations, circuit behavior, and visual explanations.

2
00:00:05,606 --> 00:00:11,005
Begin with a question, such as: where does the voltage change, or why is the branch current

3
00:00:11,005 --> 00:00:11,640
the same?

4
00:00:11,890 --> 00:00:17,902
We will use the ten volt divider first, then briefly examine model conversion in a MOSFET

5
00:00:17,902 --> 00:00:18,278
amplifier.

6
00:00:20,078 --> 00:00:21,374
Select X-ray.

7
00:00:21,624 --> 00:00:26,968
Wire color and moving markers help you follow node voltage and conventional current.

8
00:00:27,218 --> 00:00:32,935
In this series divider, current is the same through both resistors, while voltage changes

9
00:00:32,935 --> 00:00:34,161
across each resistor.

10
00:00:34,411 --> 00:00:38,284
Use the numerical labels and legend to interpret the picture.

11
00:00:38,534 --> 00:00:43,537
The animation is a teaching representation, not literal electron motion.

12
00:00:45,337 --> 00:00:48,698
Open Visual Aids and choose KCL and KVL.

13
00:00:48,948 --> 00:00:52,129
At the midpoint, current entering equals current leaving.

14
00:00:52,379 --> 00:00:56,622
Around the loop, the ten volt rise balances two five volt drops.

15
00:00:56,872 --> 00:01:02,850
Use this view to connect the displayed values to conservation of charge and energy, rather

16
00:01:02,850 --> 00:01:06,039
than memorizing a divider formula without understanding it.

17
00:01:07,839 --> 00:01:12,019
The Thermal and Power lens highlights electrical power dissipation.

18
00:01:12,269 --> 00:01:16,291
Each equal resistor absorbs the same power in this example.

19
00:01:16,541 --> 00:01:21,010
The displayed energy balance relates source power to component dissipation.

20
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:26,997
Treat the colors as a qualitative power visualization; they are not a prediction of the

21
00:01:26,997 --> 00:01:29,292
physical temperature of a real resistor.

22
00:01:31,092 --> 00:01:33,132
Use What-If to test a prediction.

23
00:01:33,382 --> 00:01:36,743
Select R two and raise it from its baseline value.

24
00:01:36,993 --> 00:01:42,557
In this view, increasing the lower resistance increases the midpoint voltage while reducing

25
00:01:42,557 --> 00:01:43,840
the series current.

26
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:49,311
Change one parameter at a time, compare the before and after values, and explain the

27
00:01:49,311 --> 00:01:50,703
direction of the change.

28
00:01:52,503 --> 00:01:55,553
The Visual Aids menu groups tools by purpose.

29
00:01:55,803 --> 00:02:01,200
Some controls are grayed out when the required devices or circuit features are absent.

30
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:05,748
A resistor divider does not need a transistor operating-region view.

31
00:02:05,998 --> 00:02:10,759
Choose an aid that matches your question, and turn it off when it no longer helps you read

32
00:02:10,759 --> 00:02:11,288
the circuit.

33
00:02:13,088 --> 00:02:17,354
For a transistor example, open the common-source MOSFET amplifier.

34
00:02:17,604 --> 00:02:23,021
The Original circuit contains supplies, bias resistors, and coupling capacitors.

35
00:02:23,271 --> 00:02:26,908
First establish the DC bias and operating region.

36
00:02:27,158 --> 00:02:32,711
Small-signal parameters are calculated around that operating point, so a signal model is

37
00:02:32,711 --> 00:02:36,128
meaningful only when the bias assumptions are appropriate.

38
00:02:37,928 --> 00:02:42,343
Use DC equivalent and Small-signal model to follow the conversion.

39
00:02:42,593 --> 00:02:46,923
In the DC view, coupling and bypass capacitors are open circuits.

40
00:02:47,173 --> 00:02:52,790
In the midband small-signal view, suitable large capacitors are treated as shorts, constant

41
00:02:52,790 --> 00:02:58,840
voltage supplies become AC ground, and the transistor is replaced by a linear model.

42
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:03,251
Follow the guided steps to connect each replacement to its assumption.

43
00:03:05,051 --> 00:03:09,212
A useful study cycle is predict, reveal, change, and explain.

44
00:03:09,462 --> 00:03:15,805
State what the aid shows, identify the equation or conservation law behind it, and check the

45
00:03:15,805 --> 00:03:16,598
result numerically.

46
00:03:16,848 --> 00:03:22,381
For waveform timing, clipping, and frequency response, continue to the Simulation and

47
00:03:22,381 --> 00:03:26,070
Measurements tutorial and use the simulator’s instrument results.
